          <strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This guide is for educational purposes only. Diabetes diagnosis and management require a qualified doctor's evaluation.
        </div>

        <h2>The Two Key Diabetes Tests on Your Blood Report</h2>
        <p>When your doctor screens for diabetes or monitors blood sugar, they order one or more of the following tests:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Fasting Blood Glucose (FBS)</strong> — blood sugar after 8–12 hours of fasting</li>
          <li><strong>Postprandial Blood Glucose (PPBS / 2-hr PP)</strong> — blood sugar 2 hours after a meal</li>
          <li><strong>HbA1c (Glycated Hemoglobin)</strong> — a 3-month blood sugar average</li>
          <li><strong>Random Blood Glucose (RBS)</strong> — taken at any time, used for quick screening</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Fasting Blood Glucose (FBS) — Normal Range India</h2>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="text-sm w-full">
              <thead><tr class="border-b border-hairline"><th class="text-left p-2 text-primary">Category</th><th class="text-left p-2 text-primary">FBS (mg/dL)</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline/50">
                <tr><td class="p-2">Normal</td><td class="p-2 font-semibold">70 – 99</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-2">Prediabetes (Impaired Fasting Glucose)</td><td class="p-2 font-semibold text-amber-600">100 – 125</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-2">Diabetes</td><td class="p-2 font-semibold text-red-600">≥ 126 (on two separate tests)</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p><strong>Important:</strong> Indians develop diabetes at a lower BMI compared to Caucasians. The Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR) recommends screening at a BMI above 23 kg/m² (versus 25 for Western populations).</p>

        <h2>What Is HbA1c and Why Is It Important?</h2>
        <p>HbA1c (Glycated Hemoglobin or Glycosylated Hemoglobin) measures the percentage of hemoglobin in your blood that has glucose attached to it. Because red blood cells live for about 3 months, HbA1c gives your doctor a 3-month average of your blood sugar levels — unlike FBS which is a snapshot.</p>
        <p>It is the gold standard for diagnosing and monitoring diabetes.</p>

        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="text-sm w-full">
              <thead><tr class="border-b border-hairline"><th class="text-left p-2 text-primary">HbA1c Level</th><th class="text-left p-2 text-primary">Category</th><th class="text-left p-2 text-primary">Action</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline/50">
                <tr><td class="p-2">Below 5.7%</td><td class="p-2 text-green-600 font-semibold">Normal</td><td class="p-2">Annual screening if at risk</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-2">5.7% – 6.4%</td><td class="p-2 text-amber-600 font-semibold">Prediabetes</td><td class="p-2">Lifestyle intervention essential</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-2">≥ 6.5%</td><td class="p-2 text-red-600 font-semibold">Diabetes</td><td class="p-2">Medical management needed</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-2">Target (if on treatment)</td><td class="p-2 text-blue-600 font-semibold">&lt; 7.0%</td><td class="p-2">ADA target for most adults</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2>Prediabetes: The Most Important Warning Sign</h2>
        <p>India has approximately 136 million prediabetics — people who are at very high risk of developing full diabetes within 5 years if no action is taken. Prediabetes typically shows no symptoms, which is why blood testing is essential.</p>
        <p><strong>The good news:</strong> Prediabetes is reversible with lifestyle changes. Studies show that losing even 5–7% of body weight and doing 150 minutes of moderate exercise per week can reduce diabetes risk by 58%.</p>

        <h2>Indian Diet Tips for Blood Sugar Control</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>✅ <strong>Choose low-GI grains:</strong> Replace white rice with brown rice, millets (jowar, bajra, ragi), or oats. If you eat rice, cool and reheat it (this increases resistant starch, lowering GI).</li>
          <li>✅ <strong>Add dal to every meal:</strong> Lentils (masoor, moong, toor) slow glucose absorption due to high protein and fiber.</li>
          <li>✅ <strong>Use bitter foods:</strong> Karela (bitter gourd), methi seeds (fenugreek) are traditional blood sugar regulators backed by clinical evidence.</li>
          <li>✅ <strong>Eat vegetables first:</strong> Starting your meal with salad or sabzi before carbohydrates significantly reduces the postprandial glucose spike.</li>
          <li>❌ <strong>Limit:</strong> Maida (refined flour), white bread, sugar-sweetened beverages, fruit juices, and refined snacks like namkeen and biscuits.</li>
          <li>❌ <strong>Watch portions of:</strong> Even "healthy" fruits like mangoes, bananas, and chikoo (which are high in sugar) if your HbA1c is elevated.</li>
        </ul>

